-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. INDIA — WARM & NATURAL
-- Angle: shared cultural roots, temple etiquette, direct
-- flights from Delhi/Mumbai/Chennai/Bangalore. Familiar but
-- different — that's the hook.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Three hours from Chennai, a whole new world. Sri Lanka feels familiar — the food, the temples, the warmth — but it does its own thing.',
    intro = 'Let''s be honest — India and Sri Lanka share more than just ocean. You''ll recognise the flavours, the hospitality, the way life spills onto the streets. But this island shrinks everything down into something you can actually wrap your head around in a week. Hill country that looks like Scotland hitched a ride with a tea plantation. Beaches that don''t need a five-star resort to feel special. Driving distances that won''t eat your holiday whole. It''s the international trip that doesn''t feel like one.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'From Delhi, it''s a 4-hour flight. From Chennai, you''re there before your in-flight movie finishes. Mumbai, Bangalore, Kochi — all under 4 hours. That means you can do a proper trip in a week, travel days included.\n\nWhat makes Sri Lanka click for Indian travellers is the mix of the familiar and the foreign. The food leans into rice, curry, coconut — kitchen staples you know — but the preparations are different. Kottu roti. Hoppers. Fish ambul thiyal. It''s recognisable but never boring. Vegetarian and Jain meals are straightforward. Your driver will know where to stop.\n\nTemple etiquette matters here, same as home. Remove shoes before entering. Cover shoulders and knees. Buddhist temples operate differently from Hindu ones — you''ll see it in the practices, not just the architecture. Ask before taking photos inside.\n\nIf you''re into Ramayana connections, there''s a whole route built around sites linked to the epic — Chilaw, Kandy, Nuwara Eliya, Ella. Your driver can connect the dots.\n\nFor families, the appeal is obvious. Private car, flexible schedule, food you''ll actually eat, and activities that span generations — elephant safaris, train rides through tea country, beaches with gentle waves. For couples, boutique hotels in the hill country punch way above their weight.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai, Bangalore, and Kochi land in Colombo daily. IndiGo, SpiceJet, Air India, SriLankan Airlines — take your pick. Flight time: 3–4 hours.\n\nVisa: Apply for an ETA (Electronic Travel Authorization) online before you fly. It''s straightforward, but do it at least a week ahead. Indian passport holders get a 30-day tourist visa.\n\nCurrency: Indian rupees aren''t widely accepted. Carry US dollars or exchange at the airport. ATMs are common in cities and tourist towns.\n\nGetting around: A private car and driver is the standard way to tour Sri Lanka. Roads are narrower and slower than Indian highways. Google Maps is optimistic — add 30% to any drive time estimate.\n\nSIM cards: Dialog and Mobitel sell tourist SIMs at the arrivals hall. Data coverage holds up well in cities and most tourist areas. Gets patchy in the deep hill country.',
    best_time = 'December to April is peak season in the south and west — the classic route through Sigiriya, Kandy, Nuwara Eliya, Yala, Galle, and the south coast. Dry, sunny, and packed.\n\nMay to September shifts the best beach weather east — Trincomalee, Pasikuda, Arugam Bay. You can still do the cultural triangle and hill country if you don''t mind afternoon rain elsewhere.\n\nOctober and November are the sweet spot for value. Fewer crowds, lower prices, decent weather if your route is flexible.\n\nFor a 4–5 day break, stick to one region. Colombo–Kandy–Hill Country or Colombo–Galle–Beach. Don''t try to do everything.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us when you''re flying in and what kind of trip you''re after. We''ll take it from there.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Can I use Indian rupees in Sri Lanka?","a":"No. Indian rupees aren''t accepted anywhere. Bring US dollars or exchange at Colombo airport. ATMs are everywhere in tourist areas."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from India?","a":"Yes — Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai, Bangalore, and Kochi all fly direct to Colombo. Flight time is 3–4 hours depending on your departure city."},{"q":"What''s the dress code for temples?","a":"Same rules as India. Remove shoes, cover shoulders and knees. Buddhist temples have slightly different protocols than Hindu ones — your driver will brief you at each stop."},{"q":"Is vegetarian or Jain food easy to find?","a":"Very. Sri Lankan rice and curry naturally includes vegetable options. Jain meals need advance notice but are straightforward to arrange through your driver."}]'
WHERE slug = 'india'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. UNITED KINGDOM — CASUAL, warm, practical
-- Angle: two-week winter escape, tea culture, weather banter.
-- Brit-friendly — talk about queuing, politeness, and the
-- glorious relief of guaranteed sunshine.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Fancy a holiday where the tea is good, the weather actually works, and nobody queues for the sun? Sri Lanka is your winter escape.',
    intro = 'Let''s cut to it: British weather is rubbish, and you know it. Sri Lanka runs on sunshine from November to April, which happens to be exactly when you need it most. Direct flights from London take about 10.5 hours — less than a flight to the Caribbean. And when you land, you get an island that packs more variety per square mile than almost anywhere else on earth. Beaches, jungle, tea plantations, ancient cities, and train rides that''ll make your Instagram look like a National Geographic spread.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka is basically Britain''s tropical cousin who got all the good genes. You''ll feel right at home with the tea obsession — they take their Ceylon tea very seriously here. The politeness level matches. The queuing is less formal but the spirit is there.\n\nWhat works for UK travellers: the two-week route. Fly into Colombo, spend a few days in the Cultural Triangle (Sigiriya, Dambulla, the ancient cities), head to Kandy for a temple visit, take the train through tea country to Ella, safari at Yala or Udawalawe, then finish on the south coast near Galle or Mirissa. It''s a classic for a reason.\n\nWildlife here is accessible. You don''t need a three-day trek to see elephants — they''re practically neighbours at some national parks. Leopards take more patience, but Yala gives you a fighting chance.\n\nThe beach finish is what seals the deal. After two weeks of moving every couple of days, three nights on the coast with a cold Lion Lager and a book feels exactly right.',
    travel_info = 'British Airways, SriLankan Airlines, and sometimes Emirates (via Dubai) fly from London Heathrow. Flight time around 10.5–11 hours direct. From Manchester, you''ll likely connect through London or Dubai.\n\nVisa: UK passport holders need an ETA. Apply online. It takes about 10 minutes and costs around £30. Do it at least 48 hours before departure.\n\nPassport validity: Your passport must be valid for at least 6 months from the date of entry. No exceptions.\n\nDriving: Sri Lankans drive on the left. Same as you. But the driving style is ... enthusiastic. Horns are for communication, not aggression. As a pedestrian, look both ways and then look again.\n\nMoney: Withdraw Sri Lankan rupees from ATMs at the airport. Avoid the currency exchange counters — the rates are worse. Cards are widely accepted in hotels, but smaller shops and restaurants are cash-only.\n\nHealth: No jabs are compulsory, but hepatitis A, typhoid, and tetanus boosters are recommended. Mosquito repellent is non-negotiable — dengue is a thing here.',
    best_time = 'November to April is the sweet spot. This is the dry season on the south and west coasts — exactly where your two-week route runs. Expect blue skies, warm sea, and zero chance of your holiday being rained off.\n\nMay to September isn''t bad — it just shifts the action to the east coast. The weather is still fine for the Cultural Triangle and hill country, but beaches on the south coast will have some rain.\n\nIf you''re travelling in October or November, you might catch the inter-monsoonal rains. Pack a light rain jacket and your patience.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us your rough dates and whether you''re more culture, wildlife, beach, or a bit of everything. We''ll build a route that actually makes sense.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do I need a visa for Sri Lanka?","a":"Yes — but it''s easy. Get an ETA online before you fly. It costs about £30, takes 10 minutes, and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"Is the driving safe?","a":"Sri Lankans drive on the left like the UK, but the style is different. Roads can be chaotic. A private car and driver is the safest and sanest option — let a local handle it."},{"q":"What''s the internet like?","a":"Good in cities and tourist areas. Most hotels have decent WiFi. Rural hill country and some national park lodges get patchy signal. Buy a local SIM at the airport for backup."},{"q":"Can I drink the tap water?","a":"No. Stick to bottled water everywhere. Most hotels provide complimentary bottles in your room."}]'
WHERE slug = 'united-kingdom'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. RUSSIAN FEDERATION — STRAIGHTFORWARD
-- Angle: guaranteed warm weather, direct flights from Moscow,
-- beach focus, no-nonsense practical advice.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Moscow to paradise in 8 hours. Sri Lanka delivers the heat, the beaches, and the kind of holiday that actually feels like one.',
    intro = 'You want warm. Sri Lanka delivers. Direct flights from Moscow and St Petersburg put you on the island in about 8 hours. No jet lag worth mentioning. No complicated connections. Just a straight shot from winter to 30°C. The beaches along the south and west coasts are the main draw — wide, warm, and backed by palm trees that actually lean over the water like they do in the pictures.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka works for Russian travellers because it doesn''t overcomplicate things. The weather is consistent. The beaches are genuine. The food is fresh and straightforward — rice and curry, fresh seafood, tropical fruit. Nothing fussy.\n\nDirect flights from Moscow (Aeroflot, sometimes charter carriers) run regularly. Flight time is about 8 hours. From St Petersburg, you might connect through Moscow or Istanbul.\n\nThe south coast is where most Russians end up — Bentota, Hikkaduwa, Mirissa, Unawatuna. These are beach towns with enough going on without being overwhelming. You can surf, snorkel, eat fresh fish, or just lie still for a week.\n\nIf you want more than beaches, Sri Lanka has wildlife parks (elephants, leopards), tea plantations in the hill country, and cultural sites like Sigiriya. But the focus stays on rest and sun.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Moscow with Aeroflot and Azur Air. Flight time 8 hours. From St Petersburg, expect a connection.\n\nVisa: Russian passport holders need an ETA. Apply online before travel. Valid for 30 days. Usually processed within 24 hours.\n\nCurrency: Bring US dollars or euros. Exchange at banks or authorised counters. ATMs are available but limits can be low.\n\nLanguage: English is spoken in hotels and tourist areas. In beach towns, some Russian is understood. Download Google Translate for backup.\n\nAccommodation: Beach hotels along the south coast range from basic guesthouses to 5-star resorts. Most mid-range places offer good value. The highest concentration of Russian-friendly hotels is in Hikkaduwa, Mirissa, and Bentota.',
    best_time = 'November to April is the dry season on the south and west coasts. Exactly where you''ll want to be. Sunshine every day, sea calm, temperatures around 30°C.\n\nMay to September is rainy on the south coast. You can still visit, but the east coast (Trincomalee, Pasikuda) has better weather during these months.\n\nFor a beach-focused holiday, November to April is non-negotiable.',
    cta_text = 'Let us know your dates and whether you want beach only or a mix with culture and wildlife. We''ll put together a straightforward route.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do I need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA. Apply online before your flight. It costs about $20 and is valid for 30 days. Processing usually takes 24 hours."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Russia?","a":"Yes. Aeroflot flies direct from Moscow to Colombo. Flight time is about 8 hours. From St Petersburg you may need a connection."},{"q":"Which beaches are best?","a":"Mirissa, Unawatuna, Bentota, and Hikkaduwa are the most popular along the south coast. Calm water, good hotels, and plenty of places to eat."},{"q":"Can I use credit cards?","a":"In hotels and big restaurants, yes. Smaller places and local shops are cash-only. ATMs are widespread but withdrawal limits vary."}]'
WHERE slug = 'russian-federation'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 4. CHINA — CLEAR & PRACTICAL
-- Angle: visa info, temple etiquette, photo opportunities,
-- shopping. Professional but not stiff.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Visa-friendly, photo-ready, and built for discovery. Sri Lanka offers Chinese travellers a compact island packed with culture, coast, and wildlife.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka is straightforward to visit from China. Direct flights from Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Kunming make the journey manageable — around 5–6 hours. The visa process is simple. English is spoken at hotels and attractions. And the island is compact enough that you can see a lot without spending your whole holiday on the road.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The island works well for Chinese travellers who want a mix of culture and nature in one trip. The ancient cities — Sigiriya, Polonnaruwa, Anuradhapura — are genuinely impressive. Built on a scale that photographs well and rewards the walk.\n\nBuddhist heritage connects the two countries in a meaningful way. The Temple of the Tooth in Kandy is a major pilgrimage site. You''ll see local devotion that''s sincere and un-staged. Dress modestly, remove shoes, and ask before pointing your camera at people.\n\nShopping is good but not aggressive. Tea (Ceylon black, green, white) is the obvious buy. Gems and jewellery in Ratnapura if you''re in the market. Batik fabrics, spices, and handmade wooden masks make practical souvenirs.\n\nWildlife parks — Yala for leopards, Udawalawe for elephants, Mirissa for whale watching — are well-organised and photogenic. Early morning safaris are the best bet.\n\nBeach towns like Bentota, Mirissa, and Galle offer relaxation without being too remote. The coastal train ride from Colombo to Galle is one of the most scenic in Asia.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Kunming to Colombo. China Eastern, Air China, SriLankan Airlines. Flight time 5–6 hours.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online before travelling. The process takes a few minutes. Check the official Sri Lankan ETA website — avoid third-party agents that charge extra.\n\nPayment: Alipay and WeChat Pay are accepted at larger hotels and some tourist shops. Carry cash (US dollars or Sri Lankan rupees) for smaller vendors and entry fees.\n\nPhotography: Sri Lankans are generally happy to be photographed, but ask first. No photography inside temple shrines with Buddha statues facing the camera — check local signs.\n\nSim cards: Dialog has the best coverage. Tourist SIMs with data are available at Colombo airport arrivals.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the best period for the classic cultural and beach route. Dry weather covers the Cultural Triangle, hill country, and south coast.\n\nMay to September: the east coast (Trincomalee, Pasikuda) has better weather during this period. The cultural sites remain accessible year-round.\n\nAvoid the main Chinese holiday weeks if you prefer smaller crowds — Chinese New Year and Golden Week see big travel numbers.',
    cta_text = 'Send us your arrival date and the kind of experience you''re looking for — culture-focused, nature-heavy, or a balanced route.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Chinese passport holders need a visa?","a":"Yes. Apply for an ETA online before travelling. It''s quick and straightforward. Check the official ETA website to avoid extra fees."},{"q":"Can I use Alipay or WeChat Pay?","a":"Alipay is accepted at larger hotels and some tourist shops. WeChat Pay is less common. Carry cash (rupees or US dollars) for smaller purchases."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from China?","a":"Yes — Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, and Kunming all have direct flights to Colombo. Flight times range from 5 to 6 hours."},{"q":"What should I know about temple visits?","a":"Remove shoes and cover your shoulders and knees. Don''t take photos inside shrines or of Buddha statues facing the camera. Ask permission before photographing people."}]'
WHERE slug = 'china'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 5. GERMANY — MORE FORMAL. Precise, detailed, structured.
-- Angle: Germans appreciate thoroughness. Talk about
-- infrastructure contrasts, efficiency, UNESCO sites, hiking.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka runs on organised chaos. For German travellers, that''s part of the appeal — the island rewards those who plan ahead and embrace the unexpected.',
    intro = 'If you''re used to German punctuality, Sri Lanka''s roads will require some recalibration. The driving style is fluid. Timetables are approximate. Trains can be delayed. That''s not a bug — it''s a feature. Sri Lanka operates on its own rhythm, and the traveller who accepts this early will have a much better time than the one who fights it. The reward is an island of extraordinary density: eight UNESCO World Heritage sites, diverse national parks, a tea-growing highlands, and beaches that stretch for miles.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka suits German travellers who value substance over surface. The cultural heritage is deep — Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa are among South Asia''s most significant archaeological sites. Sigiriya is engineering marvel and art gallery rolled into one. The Temple of the Tooth in Kandy is an active, living religious site, not a museum piece.\n\nHikers will find plenty. Knuckles Mountain Range offers serious multi-day treks. Adam''s Peak (Sri Pada) is a pilgrimage climb that rewards a pre-dawn start with views across the island. Ella Rock and Little Adam''s Peak are gentler options with big payoffs.\n\nWildlife is accessible without extreme expeditions. Yala for leopards. Udawalawe for elephants. Bundala for bird watching. The infrastructure at these parks is basic but functional — early morning safaris with experienced trackers are the norm.\n\nAyurveda is a serious draw. Genuine wellness centres in the hill country and along the coast offer treatments that go beyond spa-level indulgence. Wellworth, Barberyn, and Siddhalepa are established names.\n\nTea country around Nuwara Eliya and Haputale is visually stunning. A factory tour at a plantation like Mackwoods or Dambatenne explains the process from leaf to cup. Good for travellers who like understanding how things work.',
    travel_info = 'Frankfurt and Munich have direct connections via SriLankan Airlines and sometimes Condor or Lufthansa seasonal flights. Flight time around 10 hours. Alternatively, Emirates via Dubai, Qatar via Doha, or Turkish via Istanbul all offer one-stop options.\n\nVisa: ETA mandatory. Apply online. €30–35. Valid for 30 days. Processed within 24 hours in almost all cases.\n\nGetting around: A private driver is the most efficient way to cover ground. Public transport (buses, trains) works but requires flexibility and patience. The Kandy–Ella train is the scenic highlight — book a reserved seat in advance.\n\nMoney: ATMs accept EC-Karte with Maestro or V-Pay at most banks. Larger hotels take credit cards. Smaller guesthouses prefer cash.\n\nLanguage: English is widely spoken in tourism contexts. Street signs in Sinhala and Tamil — Google Maps works well for navigation.\n\nPacking: Light clothing for the coast, a jacket for the hill country (Nuwara Eliya drops to 10°C at night), comfortable walking shoes, reef-safe sunscreen, mosquito repellent.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the prime window for the classic Germany–Sri Lanka route covering the Cultural Triangle, Highlands, and south coast. Expect clear skies and reliable warmth.\n\nMay to September: better suited for east-coast itineraries or travellers who don''t mind short tropical downpours in exchange for lower prices and fewer tourists.\n\nOctober and November: transitional months with mixed weather but excellent value. Hotels are cheaper and main sites less crowded.',
    cta_text = 'Share your travel period and priorities — culture, hiking, wildlife, or wellness — and we''ll draft a well-paced itinerary.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do German passport holders need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before departure. The fee is around €35 and the visa is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"How long is the flight?","a":"Direct flights from Frankfurt take about 10 hours. One-stop options via Dubai, Doha, or Istanbul add 3–5 hours depending on layover."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka suitable for hiking?","a":"Absolutely. The Knuckles Mountain Range, Adam''s Peak, and the Ella area all offer excellent hiking. The best conditions are December to April, or June to September in the highlands."},{"q":"Can I use my EC card?","a":"ATMs in cities accept Maestro and V-Pay. Hotels and larger restaurants take credit cards. For local markets and small shops, carry cash in rupees."}]'
WHERE slug = 'germany'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 6. FRANCE — NEUTRAL with warmth
-- Angle: aesthetics, food culture, architecture. French
-- travellers appreciate the art of travel — talk about
-- cuisine, colonial heritage, and sensory richness.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka speaks the language of good taste. For French travellers who appreciate food, architecture, and the rhythm of real travel — this island delivers.',
    intro = 'The French know travel when they see it. Sri Lanka doesn''t disappoint. It''s an island that takes its food seriously — the cuisine is fresh, layered, and regional in ways that will feel familiar to anyone who judges a destination by its markets and kitchens. The colonial architecture — Dutch forts, Portuguese churches, British bungalows — tells a layered story. The landscape alternates between dramatic and intimate. And the pace of life allows for the kind of lingering that makes a holiday memorable.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Start with the food. Sri Lankan cuisine shares DNA with South Indian cooking but walks its own path. Fresh seafood along the coast — grilled with simple spices. Hoppers (bowl-shaped rice-flour pancakes) for breakfast. Kottu roti, a chopped flatbread stir-fry that''s the national comfort food. The spice groves near Mawanella and Matale grow pepper, cinnamon, cardamom, and cloves — you can visit and taste the difference between freshly harvested cinnamon and the dried sticks you buy at home.\n\nThe architecture is an underrated draw. Galle Fort is the showpiece — a Dutch colonial stronghold transformed into a living town of boutique hotels, cafes, and galleries. The Portuguese left their mark along the coast. The British built hill stations like Nuwara Eliya with golf courses and Tudor-style houses that feel somewhere between Surrey and the Alps.\n\nThe arts scene is small but serious. Kandyan dance performances, traditional mask-making in Ambalangoda, contemporary galleries in Colombo. The quality of craftsmanship in batik, wood carving, and jewellery punches above the island''s size.\n\nBeach culture here is relaxed. Unawatuna, Mirissa, and Tangalle attract travellers looking for good food, comfortable lodging, and water that''s warm enough to live in.',
    travel_info = 'Air France and SriLankan Airlines fly direct from Paris Charles de Gaulle to Colombo. Flight time about 10 hours. Emirates, Qatar, and Etihad offer one-stop alternatives.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Processed quickly. Keep a printed copy with your passport.\n\nEating out: French isn''t widely spoken but English menus are standard. The food scene in Colombo and Galle is excellent — do not skip the crab at Ministry of Crab. Vegetarians are well-catered for.\n\nGetting around: Private car and driver remains the most comfortable option. Roads vary from excellent to adventurous. The train from Kandy to Ella is a scenic highlight — book ahead.\n\nPhoto etiquette: Sri Lankans are open and warm but appreciate being asked before photographed. This applies doubly in religious settings.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the sweet spot for the classic itinerary covering the Cultural Triangle, hill country, and south coast. Dry weather, calm seas, and long sunny days.\n\nMay to September: the east coast is in season. Trincomalee and Pasikuda offer excellent beaches if you''re willing to shift your route.\n\nOctober and November: lower season. The landscape is at its greenest. Some rain but also more space.',
    cta_text = 'Parlez-nous de votre voyage — vos dates, vos envies. Nous construirons un itinéraire à votre image.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do French travellers need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required for French passport holders. Apply online before departing. It costs around $20 and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Paris?","a":"Yes — Air France and SriLankan Airlines fly direct from Charles de Gaulle to Colombo. Flight time is about 10 hours."},{"q":"What''s the food like for a French palate?","a":"Fresh and flavourful. Seafood is excellent along the coast. The cuisine is spicy — ask for mild versions if needed. The tea and fruit are superb."},{"q":"Is Galle Fort worth visiting?","a":"Yes. It''s one of the best-preserved colonial forts in Asia. Boutique hotels, excellent restaurants, and a working community, not just a tourist attraction."}]'
WHERE slug = 'france'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 7. AUSTRALIA — CASUAL, laid-back, direct
-- Angle: fair dinkum tone. Talk about flight connections
-- from Perth/Sydney/Melbourne. Surf, wildlife, family trips.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Fair dinkum: Sri Lanka is closer than you think. Perth to Colombo in under 7 hours. It''s like Queensland, but with leopards and ancient cities.',
    intro = 'Mate, listen. Sri Lanka is basically Australia''s tropical cousin — the one that stayed a bit wilder and more interesting. From Perth, you''re looking at a 6.5-hour flight. From Sydney or Melbourne, about 9–10 hours. That''s less than Bali from the east coast, and the island packs way more variety. Beaches, surf breaks, elephants roaming national parks, tea country that looks like the Dandenongs on steroids, and train rides that''ll make you forget you''re on holiday with a purpose.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Let''s start with the surf. Arugam Bay on the east coast is a genuine world-class left-hand point break. Best season: June to September. The south coast has good beach breaks too — Hikkaduwa, Weligama, Mirissa. You''re not going to get Indo-sized waves, but you''ll get warm water, uncrowded lineups on most days, and cheaper accommodation than Byron.\n\nWildlife here is unpretentious. Yala National Park has the highest density of leopards on the planet, and you''ve got a solid chance of seeing one on a morning safari. Udawalawe is basically an elephant buffet. Whales in Mirissa — blue whales, sperm whales — from November to April.\n\nFamilies will love the simplicity. Hire a private driver with a van, plot a loop from Colombo through the Cultural Triangle, down through tea country to the south coast, and let someone else handle the logistics. Kids love the elephants, the train rides, and the fact that every beach town has a decent pizza place if they go off curry.\n\nThe vibe is genuinely laid-back. No one rushes. Service is friendly without being fake. If you''re used to Australian directness, you''ll find Sri Lankans polite but not pushy. A smile goes a long way.',
    travel_info = 'Perth: direct flights with SriLankan Airlines or Batik Air. 6.5 hours. Sydney and Melbourne: direct flights available seasonally — otherwise one stop via Perth, Kuala Lumpur, or Singapore. About 9–10 hours total.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Costs about US$20. Takes 10 minutes. Valid for 30 days. Do it before you fly.\n\nHealth: No compulsory vaccinations, but consider hepatitis A and typhoid. Mosquito repellent is essential. The sun is fierce — bring reef-safe SPF 50+.\n\nDriving: Australians will appreciate that Sri Lanka drives on the left. The driving style is more chaotic, but the familiarity helps. That said, a private driver is still the move — let them handle the chaos while you gawk at the scenery.\n\nMoney: Aussie dollars aren''t accepted anywhere. Convert to US dollars before you arrive, or hit the ATMs at Colombo airport.',
    best_time = 'For the south and west coasts (and the classic route): November to April. For east-coast surf and beach (including Arugam Bay): May to September.\n\nFor Australian travellers, the choice depends on whether you want the classic Cultural Triangle + south coast combo (November–April) or the surf-focused east coast trip (June–August).\n\nYou can''t really go wrong — just pick the coast that matches your dates.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us your rough dates and what kind of trip you''re chasing — surf, wildlife, family adventure, or a proper lap of the island. We''ll sort the rest.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"How long is the flight from Australia?","a":"From Perth, about 6.5 hours direct. From Sydney or Melbourne, 9–10 hours direct or with one stop. Shorter than flying to Europe."},{"q":"Is the surf any good?","a":"Arugam Bay on the east coast has world-class point breaks June to September. The south coast has decent beach breaks for learners and intermediates year-round."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka family-friendly?","a":"Very. Private vehicles with drivers, child-friendly hotels, elephant safaris, and beaches with gentle waves make it one of the easiest Asian countries to visit with kids."},{"q":"Can I use my Australian bank card?","a":"ATMs accept international cards. Notify your bank before travelling. Carry some US dollars as backup. Smaller places are cash-only."}]'
WHERE slug = 'australia'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 8. UNITED STATES — FRIENDLY & DIRECT
-- Angle: two-week trip planning. Speak to the American who
-- wants culture, not just beaches. Long-haul but worth it.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Two weeks in Sri Lanka is the kind of trip that changes how you travel. Culture. Wildlife. Beaches. Real food. All in one island.',
    intro = 'Look, you''ve done the Caribbean. Maybe you''ve done Europe. Sri Lanka is different. It''s not about lying on a beach for seven days (though you can, and it''s great). It''s about an island that delivers ancient cities you can actually climb, wildlife you can see in a morning, food that''s unpretentious and excellent, and people who are genuinely glad you came. The flight is long — about 16 hours from New York with one stop, shorter from the West Coast via the Middle East. But it''s a vacation that feels earned.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka is built for the kind of American traveller who wants to come home with stories, not just a tan. The Cultural Triangle is the anchor — Sigiriya, a 5th-century rock fortress that you climb via spiral staircases and see the remains of a palace on top. Polonnaruwa, the medieval capital with colossal Buddha statues carved into rock. These are genuine wonders, not oversold attractions.\n\nWildlife is accessible. Yala for leopards. Udawalawe for elephants. Mirissa for blue whales. You don''t need a multi-day expedition — most safaris are half-day trips that leave after breakfast and have you back for lunch.\n\nThe food is a pleasant surprise. Rice and curry is the backbone, but the variety is wide — fresh seafood, roti, hoppers, kottu. Street food is safe if you''re sensible about where you eat. The fruit is phenomenal. King coconut water is nature''s sports drink.\n\nAccommodation punches above its weight. Mid-range hotels ($60–100/night) often rival $200+ rooms in other countries. Boutique places in Galle Fort, boutique villas in the tea country, eco-lodges near national parks — the options are varied and well-run.\n\nThe people are what sell it. Service in Sri Lanka is warm without being obsequious. People are helpful because they want to be, not because they''re trained to. It makes a difference.',
    travel_info = 'No direct flights from the US yet. Common routes: New York (JFK) to Colombo via Dubai (Emirates), Doha (Qatar), or Istanbul (Turkish Airlines). About 16–20 hours total depending on layover. From Los Angeles or San Francisco, Emirates via Dubai is the smoothest option.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. US$20–35. Takes minutes. Valid for 30 days.\n\nMoney: US dollars are widely accepted at hotels and larger shops. ATMs dispense rupees at fair rates. Carry small bills for tips and local purchases.\n\nJet lag: Sri Lanka is 9.5 to 13.5 hours ahead of US time zones depending on where you''re coming from. Plan a light first day in Colombo or Negombo before hitting the road.\n\nHealth: No mandatory vaccinations. Hep A and typhoid recommended. Bring good mosquito repellent (CDC-recommended DEET content). Drink bottled water only.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the prime window for a classic two-week route touching Colombo, Sigiriya, Kandy, tea country, a safari, Galle, and the south coast beaches.\n\nMay to September: this is when the east coast shines. If you''re a surfer or want a less-crowded trip, June–August on the east coast is excellent.\n\nThanksgiving and Christmas are busy. If you''re planning a holiday trip, book hotels and drivers 3+ months out.',
    cta_text = 'Drop us your dates and what you''re curious about — culture, wildlife, food, or a mix. We''ll build a two-week route that makes every day count.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Are there direct flights from the US?","a":"Not yet. The most common routes go through Dubai (Emirates), Doha (Qatar Airways), or Istanbul (Turkish Airlines). Total travel time is 16–20 hours."},{"q":"Do I need a visa?","a":"Yes. US passport holders need an ETA, which you apply for online. It costs about US$20 and is valid for 30 days. Do it before you fly."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka safe for solo travellers?","a":"Yes. Sri Lanka is one of the safest countries in Asia for solo travel. Violent crime against tourists is very rare. Normal street smarts apply."},{"q":"Can I use US dollars?","a":"Hotels and larger shops accept US dollars, but you''ll get change in rupees. ATMs are common. Credit cards work at mid-range to upscale places."}]'
WHERE slug = 'united-states'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 9. POLAND — WARM but not overly casual
-- Angle: emerging market. Winter sun. Value and authenticity.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Poland to paradise in one flight. Sri Lanka offers Polish travellers real warmth, genuine experiences, and excellent value for money.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka is becoming a favourite for Polish travellers, and the reasons are straightforward. Direct charter flights from Warsaw and Katowice during winter season. Warmth that actually works — 30°C when Poland is below zero. Prices that make a two-week holiday feel affordable. And an island that delivers more than just sun: culture, wildlife, nature, and food that tastes like someone actually cooked it.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Poland and Sri Lanka share a love for hearty food. You''ll find common ground in the rice-and-curry culture — filling, spiced, and satisfying. Fresh seafood on the coast. Tropical fruit that tastes like it was grown properly. The tea is exceptional and cheap.\n\nThe value proposition is strong. Mid-range hotels cost $40–70 a night and are often excellent. Meals at local restaurants run $3–8. A private driver for your whole trip costs less than a rental car in Europe. Sri Lanka stretches your zloty further than most Asian destinations.\n\nBeaches along the south coast — Bentota, Hikkaduwa, Mirissa, Unawatuna — are the main draw for Polish tourists. Calm water, warm sea, palm trees leaning over the sand. The infrastructure is developed enough to be comfortable but not overbuilt.\n\nBeyond the beach, the hill country offers a change of pace. The train from Kandy to Ella is one of the most scenic rail journeys in the world. The tea plantations around Nuwara Eliya are lush and photogenic. A visit to a national park like Udawalawe or Yala adds elephant or leopard sightings to the trip.\n\nPolish tour groups are common, but independent travellers will find plenty of space to do their own thing.',
    travel_info = 'Charter flights operate from Warsaw and Katowice during winter (November to April). Check with Rainbow Tours, Itaka, or other Polish operators. Regular flights via Dubai or Doha also work.\n\nVisa: ETA required for Polish passport holders. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Processed within 24 hours.\n\nLanguage: English is spoken at hotels and tourist sites. In beach areas, some tour operators speak Polish during winter season. Google Translate handles the rest.\n\nSafety: Sri Lanka is very safe for Polish tourists. Normal precautions apply — don''t leave valuables on the beach, keep cash in hotel safe.\n\nFood: Rice and curry is the daily staple. Fresh fish everywhere on the coast. Vegetarian options are plentiful. The food is spicy — ask for ''not spicy'' if your tolerance is low.',
    best_time = 'November to April is the winter sun season — coinciding perfectly with Polish charter flight schedules. This is the dry season on the south and west coasts.\n\nMay to October: fewer tourists, lower prices, but more rain on the south coast. The east coast is better during this period.\n\nFor the best combination of weather and value, March and early April are excellent.',
    cta_text = 'Daj nam znać, kiedy planujesz podróż i co jest dla Ciebie najważniejsze — plaża, kultura, dzika przyroda. Przygotujemy trasę dopasowaną do Ciebie.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do I need a visa?","a":"Yes, apply for an ETA online before your flight. It''s valid for 30 days and costs around $20. Processing takes 24 hours."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Poland?","a":"Yes, charter flights operate from Warsaw and Katowice during winter (November–April). Check with Rainbow Tours or Itaka."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka expensive?","a":"No. Mid-range hotels cost $40–70 per night. Local meals are $3–8. It''s excellent value compared to Europe."},{"q":"What''s the best beach area?","a":"Mirissa, Unawatuna, and Bentota on the south coast are the most popular. Calm sea, good hotels, and plenty of restaurants."}]'
WHERE slug = 'poland'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 10. NETHERLANDS — PRACTICAL, direct
-- Angle: Dutch travellers are independent and well-researched.
-- Speak to that. Efficient, sensible advice.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'You''ve done the research. Here''s what you need to know: Sri Lanka delivers more variety per kilometre than almost anywhere in Asia.',
    intro = 'Dutch travellers don''t need overselling. You know what you want. Sri Lanka offers it efficiently: ancient cities within driving distance of each other, national parks where you actually see animals, beaches that don''t require a boat transfer, and tea country that looks like it was designed for postcards. The island is small — about the size of the Netherlands — but packed with enough variety to fill two weeks without a dull day.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The density of experiences per square kilometre is remarkable. You can start your morning watching elephants at Udawalawe, have lunch in the hill country, and be on a beach by sunset. That''s not an exaggeration.\n\nFor Dutch travellers accustomed to organisation, Sri Lanka requires some flexibility. Roads are slower than distances suggest. Aprivate driver is the most efficient solution — it removes the guesswork, lets you set the pace, and handles the unpredictability of local transport.\n\nThe Cultural Triangle — Sigiriya, Polonnaruwa, Anuradhapura — is genuinely impressive. These sites are on the scale of Angkor Wat but without the crowds. The quality of preservation varies, but the ambition of the original builders is unmistakable.\n\nFor nature lovers, Sri Lanka punches above its size. 26 national parks. Asian elephants, leopards, sloth bears, blue whales, and over 400 bird species. The best parks are well-managed with experienced guides.\n\nBeach-wise, the south coast offers long sandy stretches with a good range of accommodation. The east coast has quieter, more spectacular beaches during the right season.\n\nTea is taken seriously. A visit to a plantation in Nuwara Eliya or Haputale is both scenic and educational — the factory tours are genuinely interesting.',
    travel_info = 'KLM offers direct flights Amsterdam–Colombo. Flight time about 9.5 hours. One-stop options via Dubai, Doha, or Istanbul if KLM doesn''t suit your dates.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Cost around €35. Takes minutes.\n\nMoney: ATMs accept Dutch bank cards widely. Credit cards accepted at mid-range hotels and above. Smaller places prefer cash.\n\nLanguage: English is widely spoken. You won''t have communication issues in tourist areas. Some Dutch is understood at tourist businesses but don''t rely on it.\n\nCycling: Sri Lanka''s roads are chaotic for cyclists. Not recommended unless you''re on a guided bike tour with support vehicles.\n\nSustainability: Sri Lanka is increasingly conscious of eco-tourism. Many hotels operate on solar power. Some national parks limit visitor numbers. Single-use plastic is still common but awareness is growing.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the ideal window for the south and west coasts and the Cultural Triangle. This is peak season — book hotels early.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. Pasikuda and Trincomalee have excellent beaches. The Cultural Triangle is still visitable with some afternoon rain.\n\nOctober and November: transitional. Fewer tourists, greener landscape, mixed weather. Good for travellers on a budget.',
    cta_text = 'Weet je al wanneer je komt? Stuur je data en interesses — dan maken we een route die precies past.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do I need a visa as a Dutch traveller?","a":"Yes. Apply for an ETA online before departure. It costs about €35 and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from the Netherlands?","a":"Yes — KLM flies direct from Amsterdam to Colombo. Flight time is about 9.5 hours."},{"q":"Is it easy to pay with a Dutch bank card?","a":"ATMs accept Maestro and international cards. Hotels and larger restaurants take credit cards. Keep cash for local shops and transport."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka suitable for independent travel?","a":"Yes, but a private driver is recommended for multi-city routes. Public transport exists but is slow and unreliable for tight itineraries."}]'
WHERE slug = 'netherlands'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 11. CANADA — FRIENDLY but not overly casual
-- Angle: winter escape angle. Longer flight but worth it.
-- Similar tone to US but more polite.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Trade your snow boots for flip-flops. Sri Lanka is the winter escape that gives you culture, wildlife, beaches, and the best tea of your life.',
    intro = 'Let''s be honest: Canadian winters are beautiful for about two weeks, then they''re just an ordeal. Sri Lanka offers a genuine alternative. November to April is the dry season on the south coast — exactly when Canada is at its coldest. The flight from Toronto or Vancouver is long (about 20 hours with a connection), but the reward is an island that delivers ancient cities, national parks, tea plantations, and warm beaches in one compact trip.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'For Canadian travellers looking for depth, not just a beach tan, Sri Lanka delivers. The Cultural Triangle is the heart of the island''s heritage — Sigiriya''s rock fortress, Polonnaruwa''s ruined city, and Dambulla''s cave temples. These are world-class sites that don''t require a separate trip.\n\nWildlife access is straightforward. Yala National Park offers the best chance in Asia to see leopards in the wild. Udawalawe is reliable for elephants — you''ll see multiple herds in a single morning. Mirissa has blue whale sightings from November to April with a 90%+ success rate.\n\nThe hill country provides a natural contrast to the coast. Nuwara Eliya, known as ''Little England'', sits at 1,800 metres and offers rolling tea plantations, cool mornings, and colonial-era hotels. The train ride from Kandy to Ella is widely considered one of the world''s most beautiful train journeys.\n\nCanadians will appreciate the friendliness of Sri Lankans. Service is warm without being pushy. People are genuinely helpful. The pace of life is relaxed — things take time, and that''s part of the charm.\n\nThe food is excellent without being complicated. Rice and curry, fresh seafood, tropical fruit. Tea is a national institution — the Ceylon tea you''ll drink here bears no resemblance to what''s sold in bags back home.',
    travel_info = 'No direct flights from Canada. Common routes: Toronto or Vancouver via Dubai (Emirates), Doha (Qatar Airways), or Istanbul (Turkish Airlines). Total travel time: 18–22 hours depending on layover.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Costs about CAD $25. Valid for 30 days.\n\nJet lag: Sri Lanka is 9.5–13 hours ahead of Canada. Plan a low-key first day. Stay in Negombo or Colombo on arrival night before heading inland.\n\nMoney: Canadian dollars are not widely accepted. Convert to US dollars before you arrive, or use ATMs. Credit cards work at hotels and larger restaurants.\n\nHealth: No mandatory vaccinations. Hep A and typhoid recommended. Mosquito repellent is essential. Bottled water everywhere.\n\nWhat to pack: Light clothing for the coast, a fleece for the hill country (Nuwara Eliya gets down to 10°C at night), comfortable walking shoes, a power bank for long travel days.',
    best_time = 'December to April is prime time. This is the Canadian winter escape window — the south and west coasts are dry and sunny, temperatures sit around 30°C.\n\nMay to September: the east coast is at its best. If you''re visiting in summer, plan an east-coast itinerary around Trincomalee or Arugam Bay.\n\nFor the best combination of weather and crowds, February and March are ideal.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us your travel dates and what excites you — culture, wildlife, hiking, or beach time. We''ll build a route that makes the long flight worth every mile.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Canadians need a visa?","a":"Yes. Apply for an ETA online before flying. It costs about CAD $25 and is valid for 30 days. Processing takes 24 hours."},{"q":"How long is the flight from Canada?","a":"About 18–22 hours with one connection. Dubai and Doha are the most common layover points. The journey is long but the destination rewards it."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka safe?","a":"Yes — one of the safest travel destinations in Asia. Violent crime against tourists is rare. Standard travel precautions are sufficient."},{"q":"What''s the best winter escape route?","a":"The classic 14-day loop: Colombo → Cultural Triangle → Kandy → Tea Country → Safari → South Coast beaches. Dry and sunny from December to April."}]'
WHERE slug = 'canada'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 12. BANGLADESH — NEUTRAL
-- Angle: close proximity, direct access. Short flight.
-- Practical travel advice for the nearest neighbour.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Close to home, far from ordinary. Sri Lanka is a short flight from Dhaka and delivers a completely different world in just a few hours.',
    intro = 'Sri Lanka is the easiest international holiday from Bangladesh. Direct flights from Dhaka land in Colombo in under 4 hours. No major time zone change. No complicated visa process. And the island offers a kind of variety that Bangladesh doesn''t have — rolling tea plantations, national parks with elephants and leopards, colonial forts, and beaches that stretch for kilometres.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The proximity makes Sri Lanka ideal for short breaks and family holidays from Bangladesh. Dhaka to Colombo is one of the shortest international flights from Bangladesh. You can leave after breakfast and be on a beach by late afternoon.\n\nSri Lanka shares some cultural touchpoints with Bangladesh — rice-based cuisine, respect for religious sites, a love for cricket. But the landscape is completely different. Hill country that stays cool year-round. National parks that feel like Africa. Beaches with surfable waves.\n\nFor Bangladeshi families, Sri Lanka is a smooth introduction to international travel. The food is familiar enough — rice and curry with fish or chicken. Private drivers remove the stress of navigating unfamiliar roads. Hotels welcome children, and most have pools.\n\nFor couples, the south coast offers boutique hotels at reasonable prices. Mirissa, Bentota, and Galle are popular choices. The hill country around Nuwara Eliya offers a romantic escape from the heat.\n\nThe shopping is decent — Ceylon tea is the obvious purchase. Quality gems and jewellery available at regulated shops. Batik fabrics and wooden masks make good gifts.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Dhaka with SriLankan Airlines, US-Bangla, or Regent Airways. Flight time 3.5–4 hours.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Bangladeshi passport holders should apply at least 3–4 days before travel. Keep a printed copy.\n\nCurrency: Bangladeshi taka is not accepted. Bring US dollars or exchange at Colombo airport. ATMs are available.\n\nFood: Rice and curry three times a day is normal. Beef is available (Sri Lanka has a significant Muslim population). Halal food is easy to find. Seafood is excellent on the coast.\n\nLanguage: Sinhala and Tamil are the main languages. English is spoken at hotels, restaurants, and tourist sites. Bengali is not widely understood.\n\nGetting around: Private car and driver is the most comfortable option. Roads are narrower than in Bangladesh but in better condition overall.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the best time for the south and west coasts — the classic route covering the Cultural Triangle, hill country, and beaches.\n\nMay to September: the east coast is in season. Trincomalee and Pasikuda are excellent during these months.\n\nFor short breaks from Bangladesh, any season works if you plan the route to match the weather.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us when you''d like to visit and what kind of trip you''re looking for — short break, family holiday, or a full island tour.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Bangladeshi travellers need a visa?","a":"Yes. Apply for an ETA online before travelling. Processing can take a few days, so apply early."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Dhaka?","a":"Yes — SriLankan Airlines, US-Bangla, and Regent Airways fly direct from Dhaka to Colombo. Flight time is 3.5–4 hours."},{"q":"Is halal food easy to find?","a":"Yes. Sri Lanka has a significant Muslim population. Halal food is widely available, especially in Colombo, along the coast, and in tourist areas."},{"q":"Can I use Bangladeshi taka?","a":"No. Bring US dollars or exchange at Colombo airport. ATMs accept international cards."}]'
WHERE slug = 'bangladesh'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 13. JAPAN — MORE FORMAL. Polite, structured, respectful.
-- Angle: Japanese standards of service and organisation.
-- Cultural sensitivity. Contrasts and connections.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Japanese precision meets Sri Lankan warmth. The island rewards travellers who value culture, service, and the beauty of things done properly.',
    intro = 'If you''re used to Japanese trains, Sri Lanka''s roads will surprise you. Give yourself more time than you think — distances here are short, but travel times are not. The island rewards patience and flexibility. What it lacks in punctuality it makes up for in warmth, variety, and depth of culture. For Japanese travellers who appreciate service, craftsmanship, and meaningful encounters, Sri Lanka offers something rare: a destination that feels genuinely different without being difficult.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka and Japan share a Buddhist heritage, but the expressions of it are different. You''ll see it in the temples — less manicured than Japanese gardens, more vibrant and alive. The Temple of the Tooth in Kandy is a living religious centre with daily ceremonies. Anuradhapura and Polonnaruwa contain some of Buddhism''s most significant archaeological remains.\n\nThe service culture in Sri Lanka is warm rather than formal. Staff will smile, ask about your family, and remember your preferences. It''s less systematised than Japanese omotenashi but more personal. Most Japanese travellers find it refreshing.\n\nThe craftsmanship is worth seeking out. Wooden masks from Ambalangoda, hand-loomed textiles, gem cutting in Ratnapura, traditional dance in Kandy. The quality varies, but the genuine articles are beautiful.\n\nWildlife experiences are excellent. Yala for leopards, Udawalawe for elephants, Mirissa for blue whales. The safari guides are knowledgeable and professional.\n\nThe food will challenge your palate less than you might expect. Rice and curry is the foundation. Seafood is excellent. The spice level is adjustable. Tea culture runs deep — a high-quality Ceylon tea experience in the hill country is a highlight.\n\nPhotography is welcome at most sites. Be respectful at temples — no selfies in front of Buddha statues, no flash inside shrines.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Tokyo (Narita) with SriLankan Airlines. Flight time about 8 hours. Alternatively, one stop via Singapore, Kuala Lumpur, or Bangkok.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Japanese passport holders can also obtain a visa on arrival in some cases, but the ETA is simpler and faster.\n\nMoney: ATMs accept Japanese bank cards. Credit cards widely accepted in cities and tourist areas. Yen is not accepted — carry US dollars or withdraw rupees.\n\nLanguage: English is spoken at hotels and tourist sites. Japanese phrasebooks and translation apps help in rural areas. Some hotels have Japanese-speaking staff during peak seasons.\n\nEtiquette: Bowing is not the custom — a smile and nod are appropriate. Remove shoes before entering homes and temples. Use your right hand for giving and receiving. Avoid public display of affection.\n\nPublic transport: The train from Kandy to Ella is a must-do. Book a first-class observation car in advance. Buses are less comfortable but cheap.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the best period for the classic route — Cultural Triangle, hill country, south coast. Dry, sunny, and ideal for photography.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. Trincomalee and Pasikuda offer excellent beaches and calm water.\n\nFebruary is ideal — dry weather in most regions, fewer crowds than December and January.',
    cta_text = 'ご旅行の時期とご興味をお知らせください。お一人様でもグループでも、最適なルートをご提案します。',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Japanese passport holders need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before travel. It''s valid for 30 days and processing takes about 24 hours."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Japan?","a":"Yes — SriLankan Airlines flies direct from Narita to Colombo. Flight time is about 8 hours."},{"q":"What should I know about temple etiquette?","a":"Remove shoes before entering. Cover shoulders and knees. Don''t take photos inside shrines or of Buddha statues facing the camera. Ask before photographing monks."},{"q":"Is the food very spicy?","a":"Sri Lankan food can be spicy. Ask for ''mild'' or ''Japanese-style'' at restaurants. Most hotels can adjust the spice level on request."}]'
WHERE slug = 'japan'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 14. ITALY — WARM, emotional
-- Angle: food, family, passion for travel. Italian travellers
-- will connect with Sri Lanka''s love for good food and life.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'If you love food, family, and places with soul, Sri Lanka will feel like home. It''s Italy with elephants and better beaches.',
    intro = 'Italians understand the good life. Sri Lanka gets it too. The island runs on fresh ingredients, long meals, loud conversations, and a genuine warmth that can''t be faked. The food alone is worth the trip — rice and curry that changes by region, seafood pulled from the ocean that morning, fruit that tastes like fruit should. Add ancient cities, wildlife, and beaches that rival the Mediterranean, and you''ve got a holiday that hits all the right notes.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The food connection is real. Sri Lankans, like Italians, build their day around meals. Rice and curry is the lunch staple — a spread of small dishes with different vegetables, proteins, and spice levels. Seafood along the coast is excellent — grilled fish, prawns, crabs, lobsters. The street food is good and safe in tourist areas. Hoppers (bowl-shaped pancakes) for breakfast. Kottu roti for late-night cravings.\n\nLike Italy, Sri Lanka has a strong sense of regional identity. Kandy feels different from Galle. Nuwara Eliya feels different from Mirissa. Each region has its own cuisine, dialect, and traditions. The variety keeps things interesting.\n\nThe family connection is strong. Sri Lankans are family-oriented and children are welcome everywhere. Hotels are used to accommodating families. Private vehicles with drivers make multi-generational travel easy.\n\nThe pace of life matches Italian sensibilities. Lunch takes time. People stop to chat. Siesta is a legitimate concept. If you''re used to Italian flexibility with schedules, you''ll adapt to Sri Lanka without stress.\n\nThe landscapes are dramatic. Hill country tea plantations, coastal cliffs, rainforest, and savannah. The train ride from Kandy to Ella is the equivalent of a beautiful drive through Tuscany — except with tea bushes instead of vineyards.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Rome and Milan with SriLankan Airlines or seasonal charters. Flight time about 9 hours. Alternatively, one stop via Dubai, Doha, or Istanbul.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Cost around €30. Very straightforward.\n\nFood note: Sri Lankan food is spicy. Italian palates not used to chilli should ask for ''not spicy'' when ordering. Most tourist restaurants are accustomed to this request.\n\nCoffee drinkers: Sri Lanka is a tea country. Good coffee is available in Colombo and tourist areas but isn''t the default. Italian coffee snobs should pack a backup plan or embrace tea for a week.\n\nWine: Sri Lanka is not a wine country. Imported wine is available at hotels but expensive. Stick with Lion Lager (the local beer) or arrack (palm-based spirit) for authentic options.\n\nGetting around: Private driver is recommended. Driving is chaotic by Italian standards — your driver handles the stress while you enjoy the scenery.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the prime season — similar to Italian summer but without the crowds. South and west coasts are dry and sunny.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. If you''re visiting in Italian summer (July–August), head to the east coast for the best weather.\n\nMarch and April are excellent months — fewer European tourists, reliable weather, and the landscape is at its greenest.',
    cta_text = 'Raccontaci il tuo viaggio ideale — quando parti, cosa ami fare, con chi viaggi. Costruiremo un itinerario su misura per te.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Italian travellers need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before flying. It costs around €30 and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"Is the food suitable for Italians?","a":"The food is fresh and flavourful, but spicy. Ask for ''non piccante'' at restaurants. The seafood and fruit are excellent. Pizza is available in tourist areas."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Italy?","a":"Yes — SriLankan Airlines flies from Rome and Milan. Flight time is about 9 hours. Charter flights operate seasonally."},{"q":"How is Sri Lanka for families?","a":"Very family-friendly. Hotels welcome children. Private vehicles with drivers make multi-generational travel easy. Elephants, trains, and beaches keep kids happy."}]'
WHERE slug = 'italy'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 15. MALDIVES — SHORT
-- Angle: travellers already in the region. Sri Lanka offers
-- more variety than a single resort island. Culture, wildlife,
-- and different beaches.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Your resort island is beautiful. But Sri Lanka is a whole country. Culture, wildlife, city energy, and a completely different coastline — all 90 minutes away.',
    intro = 'You''re already in the Maldives. You''ve done the overwater villa thing. The beach is perfect, the water is clear, and the service is impeccable. But after a few days, the island can feel small. Sri Lanka is a 90-minute flight away — close enough for a long weekend, different enough to feel like a second holiday. Ancient cities, national parks with elephants and leopards, tea plantations in the cool highlands, and a coastline that changes every hundred kilometres.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The Maldives gives you one beautiful island. Sri Lanka gives you a whole country. The contrast is the point.\n\nSri Lanka offers what the Maldives can''t — variety. You can climb a 5th-century rock fortress in the morning, watch elephants in the afternoon, and eat fresh seafood on a beach at sunset. You can explore a colonial fort town like Galle, wander through tea plantations in the hill country, and visit Buddhist temples that have been active for over 2,000 years.\n\nThe beaches are different but no less beautiful. Mirissa, Unawatuna, Bentota, and Pasikuda each have their own character. They''re not resort islands — they''re real beach towns with local life, seafood shacks, and a different energy.\n\nThe wildlife is extraordinary. Yala for leopards. Udawalawe for elephants. Mirissa for blue whales. You don''t need an expedition — just a morning or afternoon safari.\n\nThe food is a proper culinary experience. Rice and curry, fresh seafood, tropical fruit, and the best tea you''ll ever drink.\n\nFlights from Male to Colombo are frequent (multiple daily) and short (90 minutes). SriLankan Airlines operates the route. You can easily add 3–5 days in Sri Lanka to a Maldives trip.',
    travel_info = 'Multiple daily flights from Male to Colombo. SriLankan Airlines is the main carrier. Flight time: 90 minutes.\n\nVisa: Most nationalities get a 30-day ETA. Maldives residents and passport holders should check ETA requirements.\n\nGetting around: A private driver is the best way to explore. Roads are good on main routes. The island is compact enough to cover a lot in 3–5 days.\n\nWhat to do in a short trip: Colombo (city energy and restaurants), Kandy (temple in the hills), Galle (colonial fort town), or the south coast beaches (Mirissa, Bentota).\n\nMoney: US dollars accepted widely. ATMs available in cities. Credit cards at hotels.',
    best_time = 'November to April is the best time for the south and west coasts. Same as Maldives peak season — excellent weather on both sides.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. Sri Lanka still works well, with good weather on the east coast while the Maldives experiences its wet season.',
    cta_text = 'Already in the Maldives? Add 3–5 days in Sri Lanka. Tell us your dates and we''ll plan a quick round trip.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"How far is Sri Lanka from the Maldives?","a":"90 minutes by air. Multiple flights daily from Male to Colombo."},{"q":"Can I combine a Maldives trip with Sri Lanka?","a":"Easily. Add 3–5 days in Sri Lanka to your Maldives holiday. The flight is short and the contrast is tremendous."},{"q":"What can I do in a short Sri Lanka trip?","a":"Focus on one region. The Cultural Triangle, hill country, or south coast beaches. A 3-day trip works well for Galle and the south coast."},{"q":"Do I need a visa from the Maldives?","a":"Most nationalities need an ETA. Apply online. Check requirements based on your passport."}]'
WHERE slug = 'maldives'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 16. SWITZERLAND — PRECISE but warm
-- Angle: quality, nature, quiet. Talk about altitude/weather
-- contrasts. Appreciation for clean air, green hills, and
-- well-organised experiences.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'If you love green hills, fresh air, and landscapes that reward a hike — Sri Lanka delivers. Bring your sense of adventure and leave your rain jacket at home.',
    intro = 'Swiss travellers know quality when they see it. Sri Lanka''s hill country will feel familiar in the best way — rolling green hills, cool mornings, tea plantations stretching to the horizon. But the comparison stops there. Below the highlands, the island opens into dry plains with elephants, ancient cities built on a monumental scale, and a coastline that stays warm year-round. It''s a country of contrasts, and that''s exactly what makes it so compelling.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The hill country is the natural draw for Swiss travellers. Nuwara Eliya sits at 1,800 metres — cool enough for a jacket in the evenings. The surrounding tea plantations are stunningly beautiful. A walk through the hills around Haputale or Ella offers the kind of scenery that Swiss hikers will appreciate — well-defined paths, crisp air, and views that stretch for miles.\n\nBut Sri Lanka offers things Switzerland can''t. Beaches with water at 28°C. National parks where wild elephants and leopards roam. Ancient cities that predate most European cathedrals. A food culture built on fresh seafood, tropical fruit, and spices.\n\nThe quality of experiences is high. Safari guides are knowledgeable and professional. The boutique hotel scene in the hill country and along the coast is excellent — places that combine comfort with character.\n\nThe pace of life is slower than Switzerland. Things take time. Service is warm rather than efficient. Travellers who lean into this rhythm get the most out of the island.\n\nBirdwatching is excellent — over 400 species, of which 34 are endemic. Sinharaja Rainforest is a UNESCO site for good reason.',
    travel_info = 'Direct charter flights from Zurich during winter season. Otherwise, connect via Dubai, Doha, or Istanbul. Swiss and Edelweiss Air offer options. Total travel time: 10–14 hours.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Cost around CHF 25.\n\nAltitude note: Nuwara Eliya is 1,800 metres. Most travellers feel no effects, but the temperature drops to 10°C at night — pack layers.\n\nHiking: Good trails exist in the Knuckles Mountains, around Ella, and at Adam''s Peak. Guides are recommended for longer treks. Fitness levels vary — the heat and humidity make trails feel harder than Swiss hiking.\n\nMoney: ATMs accept Swiss cards. Credit cards widely accepted. Keep some cash for small purchases.\n\nLanguage: English widely spoken in tourism. German is understood at some hotels but not widely.',
    best_time = 'December to April: dry season on the south and west coasts. Also the best time for the hill country and Cultural Triangle. Clear skies and comfortable temperatures.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. The hill country is still accessible; the southern beaches get rain.\n\nFor the best hiking conditions in the highlands, February to April are ideal.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us your preferred season and interests — hiking, wildlife, tea country, or beach. We''ll build a route that meets your standards.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Swiss passport holders need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before travel. It costs around CHF 25 and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"How does Sri Lanka compare to Switzerland?","a":"The hill country looks similar — green hills, tea plantations, cool air. But Sri Lanka adds beaches, elephants, ancient cities, and tropical warmth that Switzerland can''t offer."},{"q":"Is there good hiking?","a":"Yes. The Knuckles Mountain Range, Ella Rock, and Adam''s Peak offer excellent hiking. The trails are less developed than Swiss paths — wear proper shoes and bring water."},{"q":"What''s the altitude like in the hill country?","a":"Nuwara Eliya sits at 1,800 metres. Cool enough for a jacket at night but no altitude effects for most travellers."}]'
WHERE slug = 'switzerland'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 17. MALAYSIA — NEUTRAL
-- Angle: similar climate, direct flights, close proximity.
-- Sri Lanka as an easy neighbouring getaway with a different
-- cultural flavour.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Close to home, completely different. Sri Lanka is a direct flight from KL and delivers beaches, culture, and wildlife that Malaysia can''t match.',
    intro = 'Malaysian travellers have it easy. A 3.5-hour flight from Kuala Lumpur lands you in a country that shares your tropical climate but offers a completely different experience. Different food. Different heritage. Different landscape. Same humidity, sure — but the similarities end there. Sri Lanka feels like a proper holiday in a way that a domestic trip sometimes doesn''t.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'The proximity is the big advantage. Direct flights from KL to Colombo run daily — AirAsia, SriLankan Airlines, Malaysia Airlines. Flight time is 3.5 hours. You can leave after work on Friday and be on a beach by Saturday afternoon.\n\nThe food is different enough to be interesting. Sri Lankan cuisine is rice-and-curry based but uses different spice blends than Malaysian cooking. The seafood is excellent. Kottu roti, hoppers, and string hoppers are dishes you won''t find at home. The fruit is fantastic — mangoes, papayas, rambutan, mangosteen.\n\nThe cultural heritage is distinct. The Buddhist sites — Sigiriya, Polonnaruwa, Anuradhapura — are on a scale and age that Malaysia doesn''t have. The colonial history (Portuguese, Dutch, British) adds another layer.\n\nBeaches are the headliner. Sri Lanka''s beaches — Mirissa, Bentota, Unawatuna, Pasikuda — are wider, less developed, and more relaxed than most Malaysian beach destinations. The water is warm and generally calm.\n\nWildlife is a point of difference. National parks like Yala, Udawalawe, and Wilpattu offer elephant and leopard sightings that are harder in Malaysia.\n\nFor families, Sri Lanka is smooth. Private drivers, child-friendly hotels, and plenty of activities that work for different ages.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Kuala Lumpur with AirAsia, SriLankan Airlines, Malaysia Airlines, and Batik Air. Flight time: 3.5 hours. Flights from Penang and Johor Bahru usually connect via KL.\n\nVisa: Malaysian passport holders need an ETA. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Processed quickly.\n\nClimate: Similar to Malaysia — hot and humid year-round. The dry season (December to April on the south coast) offers slightly more reliable sunshine.\n\nMoney: Ringgit is not accepted. Bring US dollars or use ATMs. Credit cards accepted at hotels.\n\nLanguage: English is widely spoken in tourist areas. Sinhala and Tamil are the local languages — no language barrier for Malaysian travellers who speak English.\n\nFood: Halal food is widely available. Sri Lanka has a significant Muslim population. Seafood is excellent. Rice and curry is the staple.',
    best_time = 'December to April: best for the south and west coasts. The classic route works perfectly.\n\nMay to September: east coast at its best. Good for surfers (Arugam Bay) and those who prefer quieter beaches.\n\nFor Malaysian travellers, the climate is familiar enough that any time works — just pick the coast that matches the season.',
    cta_text = 'Hantar tarikh perjalanan anda dan beritahu kami minat anda. Kami akan susun laluan yang sesuai.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Malaysians need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before flying. It''s valid for 30 days and processing takes about 24 hours."},{"q":"How long is the flight from Malaysia?","a":"Direct flights from KL to Colombo take about 3.5 hours. Multiple airlines operate the route daily."},{"q":"Is halal food easy to find?","a":"Yes — Sri Lanka has a significant Muslim population. Halal food is widely available in Colombo and along the coast."},{"q":"How does Sri Lanka compare to Malaysia?","a":"Different in the best ways. Different food, different cultural heritage, bigger ancient sites, and wider beaches. The tropical climate is familiar."}]'
WHERE slug = 'malaysia'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 18. SPAIN — WARM, lively
-- Angle: similar energy to Italy. Love for food, celebration,
-- vibrant culture. Talk about warmth, colour, and life.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka has the same warmth and energy as Spain — but with elephants, tea plantations, and beaches that meet the jungle.',
    intro = 'Spanish travellers will feel at home in Sri Lanka. Not because it''s similar — but because the energy matches. People are warm. Food is central to daily life. Music and celebration are part of the culture. Markets are loud and colourful. And like Spain, the island has a rhythm that rewards those who lean into it rather than fighting it. The difference? Sri Lanka adds ancient Buddhist cities, wildlife parks where leopards and elephants roam freely, and a coastline that''s warm enough to swim in every single month.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Start with the food. Sri Lankans, like the Spanish, take their meals seriously. Rice and curry is the daily ritual — a spread of small dishes that changes by region. Seafood is excellent along the coast — freshly caught and simply grilled. Street food is vibrant and safe. Hoppers for breakfast, kottu roti for a late snack, fresh fruit everywhere in between.\n\nThe people are the highlight. Sri Lankans are warm, hospitable, and quick to smile. Service is personal — staff remember your preferences, ask about your family, and engage naturally. It''s the same kind of genuine friendliness you''d find in a small town in Andalusia.\n\nThe landscape is dramatic and varied. Beaches on the south coast (Mirissa, Unawatuna) rival the Mediterranean for beauty but the water is warmer. The hill country offers cool air and tea plantations. The Cultural Triangle has monuments that are genuinely awe-inspiring.\n\nActivities are varied. Surfing at Arugam Bay. Whale watching in Mirissa. Safari at Yala or Udawalawe. Train rides from Kandy to Ella through tea country. Temple visits. Cooking classes. There''s enough variety for a two-week itinerary without rushing.\n\nLike Spain, Sri Lanka has a strong regional identity. Kandy feels different from Galle. Nuwara Eliya is nothing like Colombo. The variety keeps the trip interesting.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Madrid and Barcelona with SriLankan Airlines or seasonal charters. Flight time around 10 hours. Alternatively, one stop via Dubai, Doha, or Istanbul.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Cost around €30. Straightforward process.\n\nFood: Spanish palates will appreciate the seafood. The spice level can be adjusted. Vegetarian options are plentiful. If you miss Spanish food, a few good restaurants in Colombo serve paella and tapas.\n\nSiesta: Sri Lanka has a version of it. Many shops close between 1pm and 2pm. Lunch is the main meal. Things slow down in the afternoon heat. You''ll adapt easily.\n\nGetting around: Private driver is the comfortable option. Roads are chaotic by Spanish standards, but your driver handles it.\n\nMoney: ATMs widely available. Credit cards accepted at hotels. Keep cash for smaller purchases.',
    best_time = 'December to April: peak season on the south and west coasts. Dry and sunny — perfect for the classic route.\n\nMay to September: east coast is best. July and August (Spanish summer holiday season) work well for east-coast itineraries.\n\nFebruary and March offer the best combination of weather, lower crowds, and good value.',
    cta_text = 'Cuéntanos cuándo quieres viajar y qué te apasiona — cultura, playa, naturaleza, o una mezcla de todo. Te preparamos una ruta a tu medida.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Spanish passport holders need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before flying. It costs around €30 and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Spain?","a":"SriLankan Airlines flies direct from Madrid and Barcelona. Flight time is about 10 hours. Charter flights operate seasonally."},{"q":"What''s the food like for Spanish tastes?","a":"Seafood is excellent — fresh and simply prepared. The cuisine is spicy, so ask for ''no picante'' if needed. The fruit is outstanding."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka good for a two-week holiday?","a":"Ideal. Two weeks allows a complete route: Cultural Triangle, hill country, safari, and several days on the beach. It''s the perfect length."}]'
WHERE slug = 'spain'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 19. ISRAEL — STRAIGHTFORWARD
-- Angle: Israelis travel independently. Adventure, nature,
-- and value. Speak to the backpacker / independent traveller
-- mindset.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Sri Lanka delivers real adventure at the right price. Nature, trekking, wildlife, and beaches — without the crowds or the tourist markup.',
    intro = 'Israeli travellers don''t need hand-holding. You know how to find your own way. Sri Lanka rewards that independence. It''s a country that works for backpackers, solo travellers, and small groups who want real experiences — hiking through tea country, surfing uncrowded breaks, sleeping in eco-lodges near national parks, and eating at places where locals eat. The infrastructure for independent travel is solid, the prices are fair, and the variety is exceptional.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka works for the kind of traveller who values experience over comfort. Hostels and budget guesthouses are good. Local buses are an adventure worth having at least once. Street food is safe and cheap. The combination of beaches, trekking, wildlife, and cultural sites means you can keep things fresh for weeks.\n\nTrekking is excellent. Adam''s Peak is a pilgrimage climb that''s best done by moonlight to reach the summit at sunrise. Ella Rock offers a half-day hike with views across the southern plains. The Knuckles Mountain Range has multi-day routes for serious trekkers.\n\nSurfing is a draw. Arugam Bay on the east coast has consistent waves from June to September. The south coast has mellow breaks perfect for learning. Boards can be rented cheaply.\n\nWildlife is accessible and affordable. Yala and Udawalawe national parks can be visited as day trips. Entry fees and guide costs are reasonable compared to African safaris.\n\nThe value proposition is strong. A comfortable private room costs $15–30. A filling meal costs $3–5. A private driver for a full day might cost $50–60. Sri Lanka respects a tight budget.\n\nVisa requirements are straightforward for Israeli passport holders. ETA is quick and cheap.\n\nThe people are welcoming. You''ll find few places in Asia where locals are as genuinely helpful.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Tel Aviv with airlines like Arkia and Israir on seasonal routes. Otherwise, one stop via Dubai, Istanbul, or Doha. Flight time: 7–8 hours direct, 10–14 with connection.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Straightforward for Israeli passport holders.\n\nSafety: Sri Lanka is very safe for Israeli travellers. Normal precautions apply. The country is accustomed to international visitors.\n\nBudget: A budget traveller can get by on $30–40 per day including accommodation, food, and transport. Mid-range travel costs $60–100 per day.\n\nGetting around: For budget travel, local buses and trains are extremely cheap. For comfort, shared tuk-tuks and private drivers fill the gap.\n\nFood: Rice and curry is the staple. Fresh seafood on the coast. Vegetarian options everywhere. Street food is excellent and safe.\n\nSIM cards: Dialog offers the best coverage. Tourist SIMs with data are cheap — about $5 for 10GB.',
    best_time = 'December to April: best for the south and west coasts. Ideal for the classic Cultural Triangle → Hill Country → South Coast route.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. Surfers should target June–August for Arugam Bay. The Cultural Triangle and hill country remain accessible.\n\nFor the best value and smallest crowds, October and November are excellent — just bring a rain jacket for occasional showers.',
    cta_text = 'Tell us your rough itinerary and budget. Whether you want a private driver for part of the trip or help with accommodation, we can help without getting in your way.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Israeli passport holders need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required. Apply online before flying. The process is quick — allow 24 hours for processing."},{"q":"Are there direct flights from Israel?","a":"Seasonal direct flights operate from Tel Aviv. Year-round, one-stop flights via Istanbul, Dubai, or Doha are the most reliable options."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka good for budget travel?","a":"Very. Budget travellers can manage on $30–40 per day including accommodation and food. Hostels and guesthouses are widely available."},{"q":"What''s the best area for surfing?","a":"Arugam Bay on the east coast has the best waves, especially June to September. Weligama on the south coast is excellent for beginners year-round."}]'
WHERE slug = 'israel'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

-- ----------------------------------------------------------
-- 20. DENMARK — PRECISE but friendly
-- Angle: similar to Germany but slightly warmer. Quality of
-- life angle. Hygge by the beach. Winter sun for Danish
-- travellers who appreciate design, nature, and authenticity.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE country_pages
SET excerpt = 'Winter sun with substance. Sri Lanka offers Danish travellers warmth, nature, and genuine experiences — well-organised, human-scaled, and real.',
    intro = 'Danish travellers appreciate quality, and Sri Lanka delivers it in spades. Not the sterile quality of a five-star resort — but the real kind. Fresh food that tastes like it was grown properly. Accommodation that''s comfortable without being fussy. People who are genuinely warm, not professionally friendly. And a landscape that shifts from tea plantations to national parks to palm-fringed beaches in the space of a few hours. It''s hygge in the tropics — if hygge involved elephants and curry.',
    why_sri_lanka = 'Sri Lanka suits Danish travellers who value authenticity over luxury. The island doesn''t try to be something it''s not. The hotels are charming rather than grand. The food is honest rather than pretentious. The experiences — safari, train rides, temple visits — are genuine rather than staged.\n\nThe nature is spectacular. Yala for leopards. Udawalawe for elephants. Sinharaja for birdwatching. The hill country around Ella and Nuwara Eliya offers the kind of walking that Danes will appreciate — green, quiet, and accessible.\n\nThe design aesthetic in boutique hotels — repurposed colonial buildings, eco-lodges with solar power, minimalist beach villas — will appeal to Scandinavian sensibilities. The best places combine comfort with character.\n\nThe tea culture is a natural draw. Ceylon tea is world-class, and visiting a plantation in the hill country is a genuine experience, not a tourist trap. The factory tours explain the process in satisfying detail.\n\nFor families, Sri Lanka is straightforward and safe. Private vehicles with drivers eliminate logistics stress. The combination of wildlife, beaches, and train rides keeps children engaged without screens.\n\nWinter sun is the practical appeal. November to April is dry season on the south and west coasts — exactly when Denmark is coldest and darkest. The contrast is dramatic and restorative.',
    travel_info = 'Direct flights from Copenhagen are limited — check SriLankan Airlines or seasonal charter operators. Reliable one-stop options via Dubai (Emirates), Doha (Qatar Airways), or Istanbul (Turkish Airlines). Total travel time: 12–15 hours.\n\nVisa: ETA required. Apply online. Valid for 30 days. Cost around €35. Takes minutes.\n\nMoney: Danish kroner not accepted. Use ATMs (international cards work) or bring euros/US dollars. Credit cards accepted at hotels.\n\nClimate: Coastal areas are 28–32°C year-round. Hill country is 10–20°C. Pack layers. The sun is intense — good SPF is essential.\n\nLanguage: English is widely spoken. Danish is not, but staff at tourist hotels will understand basic English without issue.\n\nDesign note: Danish travellers who appreciate mid-century modern will find Colombo''s Barefoot and Paradise Road galleries interesting — they showcase Sri Lankan design with a contemporary sensibility.\n\nHealth: No mandatory vaccinations. Mosquito repellent essential. Bottled water everywhere. Medical facilities are adequate in cities.',
    best_time = 'December to April is the winter sun window — perfect for Danish travellers escaping Nordic winter. Dry and sunny across the south and west coasts.\n\nMay to September: east coast season. Pasikuda and Trincomalee offer excellent beaches. The hill country is still green and accessible.\n\nFebruary and March offer the best combination of reliable weather and manageable crowds.',
    cta_text = 'Fortæl os, hvornår I vil rejse, og hvad I holder af — natur, kultur, strand eller familieoplevelser. Vi sammensætter en rute, der passer jer.',
    faq_json = '[{"q":"Do Danish travellers need a visa?","a":"Yes, an ETA is required for Danish passport holders. Apply online before departure. It costs around €35 and is valid for 30 days."},{"q":"How do I get to Sri Lanka from Denmark?","a":"No direct flights from Copenhagen currently. One-stop via Dubai, Doha, or Istanbul is the most reliable option. Total travel time is 12–15 hours."},{"q":"Is Sri Lanka family-friendly?","a":"Very. Private vehicles with drivers make family travel easy. Hotels welcome children. Elephants, train rides, and beaches keep kids happy."},{"q":"What''s the best winter sun itinerary?","a":"A 14-day route: Colombo → Sigiriya → Kandy → Tea Country → Safari → South Coast beaches. December to April offers the best weather."}]'
WHERE slug = 'denmark'
  AND CHAR_LENGTH(COALESCE(excerpt, '')) < 550;

